 ],
 "caps": [
  {
   "from": 2,
   "to": 30,
   "text": "NUCFLASH."
  },
  {
   "from": 99,
   "to": 295,
   "text": "This is the top of the ladder: a possible, or actual, nuclear detonation that could create the risk of a nuclear war."
  },
  {
   "from": 301,
   "to": 445,
   "text": "Its report goes out with the highest precedence of any military report."
  },
  {
   "from": 510,
   "to": 669,
   "text": "And as far as the public record shows, the United States has never had an accidental nuclear explosion."
  },
  {
   "from": 678,
   "to": 706,
   "text": "Broken Arrow."
  },
  {
   "from": 772,
   "to": 937,
   "text": "A Broken Arrow is an accident involving a nuclear weapon that does not create a risk of nuclear war."
  },
  {
   "from": 943,
   "to": 1110,
   "text": "The Pentagon's official list counts thirty-two of them between nineteen fifty and nineteen eighty."
  },
  {
   "from": 1230,
   "to": 1433,
   "text": "In nineteen fifty-eight, after a mid-air collision on a training flight, a B-47 bomber jettisoned a nuclear weapon near Tybee Island, off the coast of Georgia."
  },
  {
   "from": 1439,
   "to": 1439,
   "text": "After ten weeks of searching, it was declared lost."
  },
  {
   "from": 1439,
   "to": 1606,
   "text": "It has never been recovered."
  },
  {
   "from": 1659,
   "to": 1802,
   "text": "In nineteen sixty-one, a B-52 broke apart over Goldsboro, North Carolina, and two bombs fell."
  },
  {
   "from": 1808,
   "to": 1963,
   "text": "Exactly how close one of them came to arming is still debated today."
  },
  {
   "from": 2029,
   "to": 2194,
   "text": "In nineteen sixty-six, a B-52 collided with its refueling tanker over Palomares, Spain, and four hydrogen bombs fell."
  },
  {
   "from": 2200,
   "to": 2263,
   "text": "Three landed near the village."
  },
  {
   "from": 2383,
   "to": 2574,
   "text": "The fourth fell into the sea, and was recovered about eighty days later, with help from a local fisherman who had seen where it went down."
  },
  {
   "from": 2695,
   "to": 2920,
   "text": "In nineteen sixty-eight, a B-52 with a fire on board crashed near Thule Air Base in Greenland, and its weapons broke apart, scattering radioactive material across the ice."
  },
  {
   "from": 2989,
   "to": 3185,
   "text": "And in nineteen eighty, in Damascus, Arkansas, a dropped socket punctured the fuel tank of a Titan Two missile in its silo."
  },
  {
   "from": 3191,
   "to": 3191,
   "text": "The fuel exploded."
  },
  {
   "from": 3191,
   "to": 3282,
   "text": "The warhead did not."
  },
  {
   "from": 3291,
   "to": 3319,
   "text": "Empty Quiver."
  },
  {
   "from": 3325,
   "to": 3480,
   "text": "Empty Quiver means a working nuclear weapon has been seized, stolen, or lost."
  },
  {
   "from": 3486,
   "to": 3584,
   "text": "It's the scenario security systems are built around."
  },
  {
   "from": 3590,
   "to": 3750,
   "text": "And there's no publicly confirmed case of an American nuclear weapon being stolen."
  },
  {
   "from": 3759,
   "to": 3787,
   "text": "Bent Spear."
  },
  {
   "from": 3852,
   "to": 3995,
   "text": "A Bent Spear is a significant incident involving nuclear weapons that falls short of a Broken Arrow."
  },
  {
   "from": 4001,
   "to": 4110,
   "text": "The best known one happened in two thousand seven."
  },
  {
   "from": 4194,
   "to": 4387,
   "text": "A B-52 flew from Minot Air Force Base to Barksdale in Louisiana, carrying six cruise missiles that still had their nuclear warheads."
  },
  {
   "from": 4393,
   "to": 4491,
   "text": "The warheads were supposed to have been removed."
  },
  {
   "from": 4497,
   "to": 4629,
   "text": "A crew spot check looked at the wrong set of missiles."
  },
  {
   "from": 4635,
   "to": 4779,
   "text": "They sat on the bomber for about thirty-six hours before anyone realized."
  },
  {
   "from": 4785,
   "to": 4940,
   "text": "The Air Force called it a series of procedural breakdowns and human errors."
  },
  {
   "from": 4946,
   "to": 5106,
   "text": "Commanders were relieved, and the Air Force said seventy airmen would be disciplined."
  },
  {
   "from": 5115,
   "to": 5143,
   "text": "Faded Giant."
  },
  {
   "from": 5263,
   "to": 5431,
   "text": "Faded Giant covers a different kind of event: an accident with a military nuclear reactor, or another radiological accident that doesn't involve a weapon."
  },
  {
   "from": 5437,
   "to": 5523,
   "text": "That's why it gets its own word."
  },
  {
   "from": 5529,
   "to": 5608,
   "text": "The ladder doesn't only track bombs."
  },
  {
   "from": 5617,
   "to": 5645,
   "text": "Dull Sword."
  },
  {
   "from": 5765,
   "to": 5956,
   "text": "Dull Sword is the bottom rung: a safety deficiency, or a minor problem with a nuclear weapon, its parts, or the equipment built to handle it."
  },
  {
   "from": 6009,
   "to": 6145,
   "text": "It sounds small, but reporting the small problems is how the big ones are prevented."
  },
  {
   "from": 6154,
   "to": 6279,
   "text": "One more word sits on top of the ladder: Pinnacle."
  },
  {
   "from": 6402,
   "to": 6609,
   "text": "Pinnacle marks the reports that go straight to the National Military Command Center at the Pentagon, like Pinnacle NUCFLASH, Pinnacle Broken Arrow, and Pinnacle Empty Quiver."
  },
  {
   "from": 6734,
   "to": 6734,
   "text": "So, from the top."
  },
  {
   "from": 6734,
   "to": 6734,
   "text": "NUCFLASH, a detonation."
  },
  {
   "from": 6734,
   "to": 6734,
   "text": "Broken Arrow, an accident."
  },
  {
   "from": 6734,
   "to": 6734,
   "text": "Empty Quiver, a missing weapon."
  },
  {
   "from": 6734,
   "to": 6734,
   "text": "Bent Spear, an incident."
  },
  {
   "from": 6734,
   "to": 6734,
   "text": "Faded Giant, a reactor."
  },
  {
   "from": 6734,
   "to": 6974,
   "text": "And Dull Sword, a deficiency."
  },
  {
   "from": 6983,
   "to": 6983,
   "text": "Which of these surprised you most?"
  },
  {
   "from": 6983,
   "to": 7164,
   "text": "Tell me in the comments."
  }
 ]
}